contentText:
In the early 2010s, Atlanta-area radio stations developed a peculiar problem: It seemed like every day, the same middle schooler, Sean Lewow , was somehow winning all of their ticket giveaways. “I had a system for doing it. It was quite literally something I’d do every single day,” Lewow, 27, says now with a grin. “Finally one day I got a call where they were like, ‘Sean, we know it’s you. Stop calling. We’ll give you the tickets, just please, for the love of God, stop pretending to be your grandmother on the phone.’”

Lewow’s determination to get closer to music has driven practically his entire life. At college, he worked his way up the ranks at the student radio station and concert committee, and he became known on campus as the kid building a creative studio and management company, Ourros, from the bunk bed in his bedroom.&nbsp;

