But it wasn’t until he met Max Motley , 28, that he figured out exactly what music company he wanted to build long-term — and who he wanted to do it with. Motley was born and raised in the Bay Area, and was similarly obsessed with all things music. A former general manager of his college radio station and intern at Paradigm (which is now part of THE·TEAM), Motley had the misfortune of graduating from college at the height of the pandemic in 2020 and looking for a job in live music. “I had to move back in with my parents, we were completely sheltered in place, and I’m applying to all these jobs post-grad,” he says. “I applied to like 50 to 100 different jobs, just trying to get a first-round interview anywhere. Nothing worked.”

In his desperation to break through, Motley turned to TikTok with the goal of showcasing his talent scouting chops by making content about his favorite artists. His page, @mostleymusic, was one of the earliest of its kind, taking the blog model and applying it to short-form. It quickly ballooned to more than 200,000 followers and led to A&R consultancies at multiple major labels.&nbsp;

Eventually, both Lewow and Motley, each in their own fashion, found their way into scouting for Darkroom CEO/founder Justin Lubliner and Interscope Records CEO/chairman John Janick . “That’s where I met Sean,” Motley explains. Still under the impression that A&R was a zero-sum game, Motley laughs that “at the time I still had this mentality of, like, everyone’s the competition, and I’ve got to take everyone down.” Lewow replies cheerfully: “The whole time I’m just like, ‘I think I made a new friend!’”

How Darkroom Founder/CEO Justin Lubliner Guided Billie Eilish to Global&nbsp;Stardom

Fast forward to February 2022, and Motley and Lewow — now assuredly friends — joined forces to start Music Soup, a joint venture with Interscope and Darkroom.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

The roster started basically by accident with country artist Vincent Mason and pop singer Katherine Li, and was built on the premise of taking bets on artists that may have otherwise gotten lost in a massive major-label structure. “We realized, ‘Okay, there are these people that are super talented; they’re coming to us for help. It’s not the type of thing right now in their stage of development where it makes sense for just a major label to do. So, you know what, maybe there’s no time like the present — maybe now is the time to satisfy the dream of starting a record label of our own,&#8217;” says Lewow.

Now, a few years later and with an expanded roster, Janick says of Music Soup: “The success they’ve achieved in just a few years with artists like Vincent Mason, Waylon Wyatt, Wisp, and hey, nothing, is a reflection of their great creative instincts, their work ethic, and the trust [Motley and Lewow] earned from both artists and partners.”

BACKGROUND

Music Soup started by signing pop artist Li and country act Mason in 2022, creating the foundation for the company’s roster. Now, four years in, it boasts six employees, including Motley and Lewow, and is headquartered in Los Angeles. According to Motley, the name Music Soup was born out of a desire to pick a name that “felt approachable and warm and inviting.” Lewow adds: “Plus, Max was in a phase where anytime I would go over to his apartment to work on Music Soup, he was exclusively eating soup, to the point where I was like, this has to be a practical joke. There’s no way you can be eating soup five days a week.”&nbsp;

The name is also “a bit of an homage to what John Janick did with [his first label] Fueled by Ramen,” says Lewow.&nbsp;

