When asked about how Music Soup works with Darkroom and Interscope, Motley explains that Darkroom’s Lubliner is “like a third partner. He is involved in everything we do and gives great mentorship. His team helps us on PR, international, anything we need.” On the Interscope side, Lewow explains that it often acts as a distributor.&nbsp;And with anything they need, “we’re able to lean on the Interscope team,” he adds. To sum it up, says Lewow: “While [all three companies] are not listed on every artist, everybody is involved to different extents in all the artists we work on.”

Lubliner says of Motley and Lewow: “Watching them grow into the executives they’ve become — and getting to be part of that journey as a partner in Music Soup — has been a privilege. They’re just great, genuine people who lead with humility, hunger and integrity.&#8221;

THE ARTISTS

Today, the Music Soup roster is an eclectic mix of talent, including Wisp, Mason, Li, Wyatt and hey, nothing. Lewow and Motley say they are not guided by genre, or even pure metrics, just potential. “When you do it this way, you can’t rest on your laurels,” says Motley. “You can’t just be like, ‘We’re only doing country, and we have already made a roadmap of what’s going to work for every country artist.’” Lewow adds: “It forces us to make sure we are always creating new strategies and that every artist’s rollout is bespoke.”&nbsp;

Wisp, for example, is a shoegaze/alt-rock artist who has featured on a song with American Football , opened for System of a Down and Deftones in stadiums, and charted at No. 10 on the Billboard Hard Rock Songs chart with her song “Your face.”

Country act Vincent Mason has an RIAA platinum-certified track, “Hell Is a Dance Floor,” and notched two songs (“Something to Lose,” a collaboration with fast-rising singer-songwriter Stella Lefty, and “Damned If I Do”) on the Billboard Hot 100 in the last year.

Chartbreaker: How &#8216;TruckTok&#8217; and Angst Powered Vincent Mason&#8217;s &#8216;Damned If I&nbsp;Do&#8217;

“We had no idea that country was going to become what country did when we signed Vincent in 2022,” says Lewow. “We just knew that he was an incredible singer-songwriter, and that we were incredibly attached to the stories that he was telling. The same thing goes for Waylon Wyatt — we loved country, folk and Americana. So it just made sense. Same thing went for Wisp.”

WHAT’S NEXT

Speaking just days before Wyatt takes the stage at the Grand Ole Opry for the first time and Mason impacts country radio with “Damned If I Do,” Lewow and Motley say they “want to take Music Soup to the moon,” as Motley puts it.&nbsp;

“We’ve been in the trenches with Vincent every single release since release one,” says Lewow. “We’re so excited that ‘Damned If I Do’ is going to radio. It’s a turning point for us, but we want so much more, and Vincent wants so much more. We want to continue growing Music Soup and our artists to be as big as they can possibly be.”&nbsp;

